{
    "background_story": "Macy was a brilliant and ambitious astronomer who spent her career studying the mysteries of the universe. Her specialty was bolometric measurements, which allowed her to calculate the total energy output of distant stars. Her groundbreaking research took her to some of the world's most advanced telescopes, from the Atacama Desert to the Mauna Kea Observatory. Macy's bolometric surveys of the cosmos led to a series of remarkable discoveries, including the detection of a previously unknown class of variable stars. Her work shed new light on the life cycles of celestial bodies and earned her numerous accolades within the scientific community. Despite the complexity of her research, Macy was known for her ability to explain complex concepts in simple terms, inspiring a new generation of stargazers and scientists.",
